#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

/*
Key observation:

After all vertices are colored, white vertices survive iff they have no black neighbor.
So the set of surviving white vertices must be an independent connected component
separated from all black vertices; in a tree this means Takahashi wins iff he can ensure
that at least one edge has both endpoints white? Not enough.

Game-theoretic reformulation:
A white vertex survives exactly when all its neighbors are also white.
Hence the set of surviving white vertices is a union of connected components consisting
entirely of white vertices, with no black neighbor outside. In a connected tree, this means
if any white survives, then in fact there is a connected induced subtree all of whose boundary
is empty; therefore it must be the whole tree unless some adjacent black exists. More directly,
a vertex survives iff its closed neighborhood is all white.

This classic AtCoder game reduces to:
Takahashi wins iff the tree is NOT a "second-player win" in the vertex picking game,
which on trees is equivalent to whether the first player can take a vertex from some maximum
independent set? The known result for this exact problem is:
Aoki wins iff the tree has a perfect matching.
Otherwise Takahashi wins.

Reason:
- If the tree has a perfect matching, Aoki can always respond by taking the matched partner
  of Takahashi's move, ensuring every white vertex has an adjacent black matched partner.
- If the tree has no perfect matching, by König-type/tree argument there exists a vertex
  uncovered by every maximum matching strategy; Takahashi can force at least one unmatched
  white vertex to remain. On trees this criterion is exact.

So we only need to determine whether the tree has a perfect matching.

Tree DP:
dp0[v] = whether subtree rooted at v can be perfectly matched with v matched to one of its children
         or parent? Better use:
f0[v]: subtree of v can be perfectly matched, with v NOT matched to parent.
f1[v]: subtree of v can be perfectly matched, with v matched to parent.
Then:
f1[v] = all children u must satisfy f0[u].
f0[v] = choose exactly one child x matched with v, so x must be in state f1? Actually if x is matched
        with v, then inside x-subtree x is matched to parent => f1[x].
        All other children u must be internally perfectly matched without parent => f0[u].

Leaves: f1[leaf]=true, f0[leaf]=false.
Root has no parent, so tree has perfect matching iff f0[root] is true.
*/

int N;
vector<vector<int>> g;
vector<int> parent_;
vector<char> f0, f1;

void dfs(int v, int p) {
    parent_[v] = p;
    for (int u : g[v]) if (u != p) dfs(u, v);

    bool all_f0 = true;
    for (int u : g[v]) if (u != p) {
        if (!f0[u]) all_f0 = false;
    }
    f1[v] = all_f0;

    bool can0 = false;
    for (int x : g[v]) if (x != p) {
        if (!f1[x]) continue;
        bool ok = true;
        for (int u : g[v]) if (u != p && u != x) {
            if (!f0[u]) {
                ok = false;
                break;
            }
        }
        if (ok) {
            can0 = true;
            break;
        }
    }
    f0[v] = can0;
}

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    cin >> N;
    g.assign(N + 1, {});
    for (int i = 0; i < N - 1; ++i) {
        int a, b;
        cin >> a >> b;
        g[a].push_back(b);
        g[b].push_back(a);
    }

    if (N % 2 == 1) {
        cout << "First\n";
        return 0;
    }

    parent_.assign(N + 1, -1);
    f0.assign(N + 1, 0);
    f1.assign(N + 1, 0);

    dfs(1, 0);

    if (f0[1]) cout << "Second\n";
    else cout << "First\n";

    return 0;
}
